WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
What it means for engineers and PM leaders.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:07.128
Four implications follow from the evidence, not from anyone’s forecast.

3
00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:10.907
The bottleneck moves to specification and verification.

4
00:00:11.187 --> 00:00:17.864
Every careful study finds that the time saved writing is partly spent reviewing, correcting, and integrating.

5
00:00:18.144 --> 00:00:26.912
Teams that get large gains invest in tests, review practices, and clear specs, which is the checks-and-balances theme of this whole program.

6
00:00:27.192 --> 00:00:29.091
Entry-level work changes first.

7
00:00:29.371 --> 00:00:44.539
The hiring evidence is a reason to design how juniors learn (pairing, review of AI-generated work, tasks that build judgment) rather than to stop hiring them; someone has to become the senior engineer who can verify the agent.

8
00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:46.099
Measure your own team.

9
00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:49.621
External effects range from negative to very large.

10
00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:56.429
A small, pre-registered pilot (the exercise above, with more tasks) is cheap and beats any vendor claim.

11
00:00:56.709 --> 00:00:59.205
Expect the tools to keep changing the answer.

12
00:00:59.485 --> 00:01:04.008
METR’s study broke because the treatment improved faster than the experiment ran.

13
00:01:04.288 --> 00:01:08.811
Plan to re-measure every six months, the same way you rerun a model eval.
